> Like: "You have found a time portal. Do you wish to enter? [yn]
                             [...]

No, this is an ancient mall, not a modern one.  That is why the mall cops
are armed with wands of magic missile instead of .38s.

The way it was envisioned it would be a natural extension of nethack as
it is, with lots of safeguards to make sure that discovering the mall level
doesn't give away the game (and the challenge).

I don't want to see it become a diversion from the stream.  It *would* be
different from other levels (I hadn't thought it should include regular
monsters, except perhaps the Wiz, if he should choose to venture there -
no decent mall cop is going to allow vampires and co. to roam unmolested
in *his* mall).  ;^)

I thought the consumers would be fun.  They'd be pushy and impatient
(too stupid to realise that you carry 3' of razor sharp steel that you
aren't afraid to use).  They'd get in your way in the halls and tell
you to get out of their way, etc.  Sort of like the stooges, harmless
but obnoxious.

Plus the idea of new shops on the same level that offer services instead
of items.  Give you coders something new to do with that #chat command ;^).

I think the corridors between the shops should be coded like one huge
room that is lit (malls always have good lighting in the halls, right?).
Then each shop lights up when you go in.

There should be an information booth where you can apply for a credit card
and learn to be very deferential to the wizard in the magic shop (if he
doesn't like your attitude he may get started muttering, and you know what
that means ;^).  Give him some money and apologize and grovel a little bit -
he might stop muttering at you ;^).

Also be nice to the teacher in the martial arts studio - he is the strongest
fighter in the nethack world.  But he is not a pushy guy.  Just don't be too
obnoxious to him.  He takes it easy on you when you are taking a class from
him.  But he can hit you right through that -30 armor class if he so
chooses...

I must say the idea of a "wishing fountain" occurred to me as well.  I think
that you should be allowed one wish that is actually unconditionally granted
(why not?).  Throw in the wishing coin (purchased at the information desk
for $$$, of course - you can't throw the gold coins).  Make your wish.  But,
like all wishing wells, you don't get what you wished for right away.  You
get your wish some unspecified amount of time later.  Actually, you will find
your wish somewhere between level 25 and hell, laying on the ground,
unidentified. Who says getting a wish has to be easy?  ;^)  Once you get below
25 you will find yourself looking carefully for a pair of shoes that might
turn out to be those blessed +3 speed boots you wished for ;^).  Just for
grins, you might find that there are more pairs of shoes than normal on those
levels after your wish.   ;^)

I think wishing can be fun (like an easter egg hunt) without making the game
a pushover.  What do you think?

